    # 28 — p118 tips: noisy pipes at night
    dict(
        pillar="tips",
        topic="Pipes banging and ticking at night — causes and fixes",
        slug="central-heating", suffix="noisy-pipes",
        hint="asset:pipe", itype="asset", banner="upper",
        caption="Pipes banging at night? 🔊",
        cta=None, extra_cta="Save this for the next 2am clank.",
        fb="🔊 Pipes that tick, creak or BANG at night — here's what your house is trying to say:\n\n🔥 Ticking as heating warms up or cools down = pipes expanding against joists. Annoying, rarely serious.\n💥 A single thud when a tap or washing machine shuts off = water hammer. A valve slamming shut.\n💨 Tapping or gurgling from radiators = air in the system. Bleed them.\n🫖 Rumbling from the boiler itself = kettling — scale or sludge. That one deserves attention.\n\nMost night noises are harmless. The boiler rumble isn't. Know the difference and you'll sleep better — literally.",
        ig="What your pipes are saying at 2am 🔊\n\n🔥 Ticking as heating warms/cools = pipes expanding against joists. Annoying, rarely serious\n💥 One thud when a tap or machine shuts off = water hammer — a valve slamming\n💨 Tapping/gurgling from rads = air in the system. Bleed them\n🫖 Rumbling from the boiler = kettling — scale or sludge. That one matters\n\nMost night noises are harmless. The boiler rumble isn't. Know the difference, sleep better.",
        tw="Pipes at 2am, translated 🔊\n\nTicking = expansion, harmless\nThud after a tap shuts = water hammer\nGurgling rads = air, bleed them\nRumbling BOILER = kettling — that one needs a look",
        gb="Night-time pipe noises have distinct causes, and telling them apart saves worry. Ticking or creaking as the heating warms up and cools down is thermal expansion — pipes moving slightly against joists, irritating but rarely serious. A single thud when a tap or washing machine shuts off is water hammer, caused by a valve closing abruptly, and can be cured with an arrestor or pressure adjustment. Tapping or gurgling from radiators means trapped air that bleeding will release. The noise that genuinely warrants attention is a rumbling from the boiler itself — kettling, the sound of water boiling against scale or sludge, which shortens the boiler's life. Heating diagnostics across Southampton.",
        tags=["#PlumbingTips", "#NoisyPipes", "#CentralHeating", "#HomeTips"],
    ),
